.TH VERSE 1 "2026-07-31" "bashified" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
verse \- carry a scanned book from the drop share to a citable excerpt
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B verse
[\fIlist\fR|\fIstatus\fR]
.br
.B verse list
[\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B verse status
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B verse accept
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B verse ingest
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B verse ocr
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B verse snapshot
[\fIslug\fR]
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B verse publish
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B verse reap
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B verse run
.br
.B verse proof
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B verse health
[\fB\-\-notify\fR] [\fB\-\-no-freshness\fR]
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B verse perms
.br
.B verse
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B verse
is the verb of
.BR bibliothecaire .
It moves one artifact along a single line: a PDF appears in a write\-only
drop share, and what remains at the far end is a bounded excerpt a quote can
cite. Every stage of that line is a subcommand of this utility, run in the
order the SYNOPSIS lists them.
.PP
The line has one direction and five resting places on disk. A subcommand
moves items from one to the next; it never moves them back.
.TP
.I incoming/
The write\-only share. Bytes, nothing more. Nothing here is trusted, read as
a document, or acted on until
.B accept
looks at it.
.TP
.I accepted/
What survived
.BR accept :
stable in size, carrying PDF magic, owned by the local user.
.TP
.I work/
Extracted text. Working material, discarded at snapshot.
.TP
.I rejected/
Not a PDF, or a PDF nothing here opens. Retained permanently, never reaped.
.TP
.I published/
The read\-only share consumers read: one real page per scan that has one,
plus the quote stream.
.PP
Each item's position on that line is recorded in a ledger, which is the only
place the position is recorded.
.B status
reads it;
.BR accept ,
.BR ingest ,
.BR snapshot ,
.B reap
and
.B ocr
take an exclusive lock before writing it, so a hand\-typed subcommand and a
timer that fire together do not lose each other's writes.
.B publish
takes no lock, because it only reads the ledger.
.SS Subcommands
.TP
.B list
Print every subcommand and whether tooling backs it. Honours
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.B status
Report what sits at each position on the line right now. Reads; changes
nothing.
.TP
.B accept
.I incoming/
to
.IR accepted/ .
An item is accepted when its size has stopped changing and its first bytes
are PDF magic. Anything else lands in
.IR rejected/ .
.TP
.B ingest
.I accepted/
to
.IR work/ .
Extracts a text layer. A scan carrying no text layer is parked at
.B needs\-ocr
rather than failed, because the item is intact and only the reading of it is
missing.
.TP
.B ocr
Read the items parked at
.BR needs\-ocr .
Slow: roughly 50 seconds per page, measured, so a long book runs for hours.
It carries its own lock and its own timer for that reason, and
.B run
excludes it. An item this cannot read ends at
.BR ocr\-failed ,
which is retained and never reaped.
.TP
.B snapshot
Build the retained fair\-use excerpt for each item that has pinned quotes:
bounded context around the passages actually cited, and nothing else. Given a
slug, does one item.
.TP
.B publish
Regenerate
.IR published/ .
.TP
.B reap
Delete an original. It deletes only what is snapshotted
.I and
proven present in a backup, and it proves the backup by probing it, not by
consulting a record that a backup once happened.
.TP
.B run
.BR accept ,
.BR ingest ,
.BR snapshot ,
.BR publish ,
.B reap
in that order: the drain. Publish precedes reap because a page is liftable
only while a PDF remains to lift it from.
.TP
.B proof
Probe the backup route alone and say whether
.B reap
is able to delete anything. Answering that question without deleting is the
whole subcommand.
.TP
.B health
Re\-probe the live line end to end and report what was seen: the external
readers on
.BR PATH ,
the configured language, the shares, the backup route. It records an
attestation only when every probe passed.
.TP
.B perms
Take ownership of files the share's client left unreadable. Requires root.
.PP
Every stage fails loud. A stage that finds nothing to do succeeds; a stage
that cannot do its job \- an unreadable directory, a missing external reader,
an unreachable backup host \- exits nonzero with the reason on standard
error. A line that reports "clean" while blind is how an original gets
deleted that no backup holds.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable output. Honoured by
.BR list .
Requested of any other subcommand it exits 4 rather than being dropped,
because a flag silently ignored is a caller lied to.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Results on standard output, commentary suppressed. Honoured everywhere.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Usage summary, including the exit vocabulary and the flags below.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the utility name and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-notify
With
.BR health :
on a failed probe, tell the desktop and the operations project. Alone it is a
usage error.
.TP
.B \-\-no-freshness
With
.BR health :
skip the "has anyone looked lately" probe. This exists for the first run,
before any attestation exists to be stale.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of flags; everything after is a positional argument.
.SH THE COST BOUNDARY
.B verse does not spend money.
No subcommand of it spends, so it carries no
.B \-\-summon
flag: passing one is a usage error naming the absence rather than a flag
quietly accepted.
.B \-\-help
answers the question "can this cost me anything?" with no, on its own, with
no reference to this page.
.PP
What
.B ocr
spends is time on this machine \- hours of local processor, at roughly 50
seconds per page \- and that is the reason it sits behind its own timer
instead of inside
.BR run ,
not a money boundary. The readers it drives are installed locally from the
distribution's packages; nothing on this line reaches a paid external
service, which is why
.B proof
probes a host on the local network rather than a bill.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VERSE_INTAKE_ROOT
Override the root the whole line operates under. Test harnesses set it so a
run touches a scratch tree instead of the live shares.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The promise was kept. A stage that found nothing to do is one of these.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error; the caller is wrong. An unknown subcommand, a flag attached to a
subcommand that does not take it, and
.B \-\-summon
are each one of these.
.TP
.B 4
GAP: the tooling to keep this promise does not exist here.
.B \-\-json
asked of a subcommand that does not produce it exits this way.
.TP
.B 5
BROKEN: it ran with valid arguments and failed to finish. A stage that had
work, attempted it, and left it undone is this.
.TP
.B 6
BLIND: it cannot read the thing it was asked to report on \- an unreadable
directory, a missing external reader, an unreachable backup host. This is "I
cannot see", never "nothing to report", and it is never reported as a pass,
because a blind pass is what deletes an unbacked original.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/verse
This utility.
.TP
.I bin/intake.py
The implementation every subcommand above runs.
.TP
.I intake.json
The configured roots, the language, and the backup route. Read here and
nowhere else.
.TP
.I excerpts/
The retained snapshots. Untracked.
.TP
.I quotes/quotes.json
The pinned quotes a snapshot is bounded around.
.SH EXAMPLES
List the subcommands and whether tooling backs each:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ verse list
MECHANIZED    accept
MECHANIZED    health
MECHANIZED    ingest
MECHANIZED    list
MECHANIZED    ocr
MECHANIZED    perms
MECHANIZED    proof
MECHANIZED    publish
MECHANIZED    reap
MECHANIZED    run
MECHANIZED    snapshot
MECHANIZED    status
.fi
.RE
.PP
Name the utility:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ verse --version
verse (bashified)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Authorise spending on a utility that cannot spend. The flag is refused by
name rather than accepted and ignored:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ verse --summon
verse: --summon: this utility never spends; the flag does not exist here
$ echo $?
2
.fi
.RE
.PP
Ask a subcommand for machine\-readable output it does not produce. The flag
is refused before any work starts, so a mutating subcommand mutates nothing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ verse reap --json
verse: GAP: --json: not wired for this subcommand
verse: no tooling exists for this yet; see GAPS.md
$ echo $?
4
.fi
.RE
.PP
Ask which subcommands have tooling behind them, for a script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ verse list --json
{"accept":"MECHANIZED","health":"MECHANIZED","ingest":"MECHANIZED","list":"MECHANIZED","ocr":"MECHANIZED","perms":"MECHANIZED","proof":"MECHANIZED","publish":"MECHANIZED","reap":"MECHANIZED","run":"MECHANIZED","snapshot":"MECHANIZED","status":"MECHANIZED"}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B proof
asks whether the drain is able to delete anything, without deleting. It is
deliberately NOT shown here as a doctest: its answer depends on whether a
completed
.BR gardien (1)
snapshot is reachable, which is a fact about the backup host and not about
this utility. Measured 2026\-07\-31, it exits 5 and names the reason \- no
dated directory under the backup route carries
.BR .gardien-snapshot-complete ,
so
.B reap
is currently unable to delete any original. That is the reap gate holding, and
it is the correct direction to fail in.
.SH HISTORY
Written 2026\-07\-31, before
.I bin/verse
existed, which is the method rather than an oversight: this page is the
promise, and the implementation is scored against it. The line it describes
runs today under
.IR bin/intake.py ,
where the twelve subcommands above are twelve flags on one program; naming
them subcommands is the change this page asks for and the only one it asks
for.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR make (1),
.BR rsync (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR logrotate (1),
.BR test (1),
.BR flock (1)
.PP
Lineage: the staged line with one resting place per stage is
.BR make (1)-like,
in that a subcommand does the work its stage still owes and succeeds having
done nothing when the stage is current;
.B reap
follows
.BR logrotate (1)
in deleting only what a downstream copy demonstrably holds; the exclusive
ledger lock is
.BR flock (1)
semantics; the exit vocabulary follows
.BR test (1)
in reserving 0 for success and giving distinct nonzero codes distinct
meanings.
